 "answer": {
  "answer": "Only two challenging cases were confirmed, not the five the goal asked for: 02eadd92 at 1.2x speed with the crossing car 0.5 s early (S1), and 054b5901 at 1.2x with the recorded timing (S2). Every repeat at both settings failed, and the 90% ranges put their failure rates above 0.5. Neither case shows the crossing conflict the question is about. In both, the policy ignored a RIGHT route command and drifted left. On 02eadd92 it hit the slow retimed bus in the next lane over. On 054b5901 it ran onto the median with no other actor involved, which is the kind of failure the plan said to ignore. Each scene was tested at only one speed and timing, so the study cannot say whether speed or timing caused these failures. Every one of these failures also had the ego more than 3.5 m off the recorded path. The triage blames the policy, but the simulator's rendering out there may play a part. No other scene or setting failed.",
  "findings": [
   {
    "claim": "On 02eadd92 at 1.2x speed with the crossing bus 0.5 s early, every repeat ended with the ego's front hitting the bus. The cause was not a crossing conflict: the policy ignored the RIGHT command, drifted left across lanes and ran into the slow or stopped bus in the lane to its left, slowing only a little.",
    "settings": [
     "S1"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "On 054b5901 at 1.2x speed with the recorded timing, every repeat ended with the ego leaving the road at the median. It stayed in the far-left lanes against a RIGHT command, and the crossing car played no part. This is a route-following failure, not an effect of the crossing car's timing.",
    "settings": [
     "S2"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "Every failure at both confirmed settings is flagged as a possible artifact, because the ego was more than 3.5 m off the recorded trajectory. The triage puts the fault on the policy's drift, but rendering that far off the recording may affect what the policy saw.",
    "settings": [
     "S1",
     "S2"
    ]
   },
   {
